<onlyinclude>A '''voltage regulator''' is a power-conditioning feedback system whose small-signal dynamics, conversion losses and operating constraints determine whether a specified rail remains within tolerance under disturbances.</onlyinclude>

== Regulation and control ==
For a conventional negative-feedback regulator, the sensed voltage is ''V''<sub>FB</sub> = β''V''<sub>out</sub>. In steady state with sufficiently high loop gain, ''V''<sub>out</sub> ≈ ''V''<sub>ref</sub>/β. Finite gain, reference error, offsets and load-dependent impedance cause deviations. Stability depends on the open-loop transfer function and compensation; a DC setpoint calculation cannot establish phase margin or load-step behavior. Real switching controllers may use voltage-mode, current-mode or hysteretic control with distinct dynamics.

== Linear regulation: thermal and headroom constraints ==
For a series linear regulator with negligible ground current, ''P''<sub>loss</sub> ≈ (''V''<sub>in</sub> − ''V''<sub>out</sub>)''I''<sub>out</sub> and η ≈ ''V''<sub>out</sub>/''V''<sub>in</sub>. At 12 V in, 5 V out and 1 A, output power is 5 W, input power is approximately 12 W, heat is 7 W and idealized efficiency is 41.7%. If θ<sub>JA</sub> = 20 °C/W under the relevant board conditions, the first-order junction rise is 140 °C: an illustration of thermal infeasibility, not a safe operating-point recommendation. Ground current, dropout, thermal shutdown, package/PCB thermal paths and transient stresses require datasheet-specific analysis.

== Switching conversion: ideal relations and nonideal behavior ==
For an ideal continuous-conduction buck converter, ''V''<sub>out</sub> = ''D V''<sub>in</sub>, where ''D'' is duty ratio. At 12 V to 5 V, ''D'' ≈ 0.417. For a simple buck stage, the idealized inductor ripple magnitude is Δ''I''<sub>L</sub> ≈ (''V''<sub>in</sub> − ''V''<sub>out</sub>)''D''/(''L f''<sub>s</sub>). At 10 µH and 500 kHz, the estimate is 0.583 A peak-to-peak. This assumes ideal components, fixed-frequency PWM and continuous conduction; discontinuous operation, switching dead time, losses and control modes modify the relationship. Capacitor ESR, capacitance and layout affect output ripple, while switching edges contribute conducted and radiated emissions.

== Stability, transients and noise ==
The output LC network introduces resonant dynamics. Feedback compensation must be designed for the chosen topology, operating mode, output network and load range. Datasheet stability constraints and measurement of gain/phase response are preferable to a generic crossover-frequency rule. PSRR is frequency dependent and must not be confused with output noise or load-transient deviation. LDO post-regulation can reduce noise, but modern filtered buck converters can also serve low-noise rails.

== Provenance and limitations ==
The equations above are introductory circuit approximations, not measured performance or device-specific guarantees. No numerical stability margin, ripple spectrum or junction temperature can be certified without component parameters, layout and operating conditions. Primary engineering references:
* Analog Devices, [https://www.analog.com/en/resources/app-notes/2020/05/12/19/12/an-140.html AN-140], linear versus switching regulator power losses.
* Texas Instruments, [https://www.ti.com/document-viewer/lit/html/slvafj5 SLVAFJ5], buck operation and duty-cycle limitations.
* Texas Instruments, [https://www.ti.com/product/TPS65288 TPS65288 datasheet], peak-current-mode compensation and protection.
* Analog Devices, [https://www.analog.com/en/resources/analog-dialogue/articles/applying-low-dropout-regulators.html Applying low-dropout regulators], dropout and transient criteria.
* Texas Instruments, [https://www.ti.com/document-viewer/lit/html/SLUAAW3/GUID-C77C7F0E-8CFA-4E42-BB7D-F3347F516E3B SLUAAW3], low-noise point-of-load buck design.
